Patch for release-plz 0.4.3 (tag release-plz-v0.4.3), applied by
.github/actions/setup-release-plz before it builds the release tool.

Problem: in git-only mode release-plz rebuilds the workspace at each
package's newest release tag and looks the package up there by its
current name. A package renamed after its tag was created has its old
name in that historical workspace, so the lookup fails with
`cannot find package "<name>" in workspace`. This repository's tags
git-wt-cli-v0.1.0, sniff-detection-cli-v0.1.0 and darkmatter-lsp-v0.1.0
point at a commit where those packages were worktree-cli, sniff-cli and
dmls. Published tags are never moved, so the tool is patched instead.

Change: when no released workspace member has the current name, the
member whose manifest has the same path relative to the workspace root
is the package. The current-name match stays first; when neither
matches, the original error is returned (never an initial release).
The released package is stored under its current name, and the
Cargo.lock comparison finds it the same way. Unit tests cover the three
renamed shapes, the current-name path, a name-or-path miss, and the
lockfile comparison.

Drop this file (and the build-from-source step) once an upstream
release resolves renamed packages itself.

